Use structured output when application code needs a typed result such as a classification, extraction, or UI description. A JSON request in prose can still omit fields, add invalid values, or emit malformed JSON. A provider's schema-constrained mode can enforce the supported JSON Schema shape, but the application must still handle refusals, transport errors, semantic mistakes, and schema evolution. Use function calling instead when the model is selecting or parameterizing an application capability. Parse and validate at the boundary, keep the schema narrow, and do not confuse structural validity with factual correctness.

```ts caption="A TypeScript type alone is not runtime validation; validate structure and domain constraints at the boundary."
type Ticket = { category: 'hardware' | 'software' | 'other'; confidence: number };

function acceptTicket(value: unknown): Ticket {
  if (typeof value !== 'object' || value === null) throw new Error('invalid ticket');
  const ticket = value as Record<string, unknown>;
  if (!['hardware', 'software', 'other'].includes(ticket.category as string)) {
    throw new Error('invalid category');
  }
  if (typeof ticket.confidence !== 'number' || ticket.confidence < 0 || ticket.confidence > 1) {
    throw new Error('invalid confidence');
  }
  return { category: ticket.category as Ticket['category'], confidence: ticket.confidence };
}
```
